{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed56627177/5/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"758abdf0\" lines=\"54-69\">\n<description>\nAdvance short marker bars away from the full edge bar, turning completed two-pixel markers into edge-color marks at the opposite side.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One non-background color forms a complete one-cell-thick bar on exactly one outer edge.\n- The other foreground color appears only in the first two cells inward from that bar, lane by lane.\n\nRule\n- In each lane perpendicular to the edge bar, a one-pixel marker adjacent to the bar grows one more cell inward.\n- A two-pixel marker in those first two inward cells is cleared back to background.\n- The cleared two-pixel marker is replaced by a two-pixel mark of the edge-bar color at the opposite side of the same lane.\n\nKey insight\n- The full edge bar defines both the orientation and the direction of the local two-step marker cycle.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7ec998c9\" lines=\"14-28\">\n<description>\nDraw a blue folded corner-to-corner route through the lone marker's column.\n\nInput structure:\n- A rectangular grid filled with one background color.\n- Exactly one non-background marker pixel; blue is absent from the input.\n\nRule:\n- Preserve the marker and repaint background pixels in its column blue from top to bottom.\n- If the marker is in the unique center column, connect that column to the top-right and bottom-left corners.\n- Otherwise, connect the column to the top-left and bottom-right corners.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker's column is the route spine; its central vs off-center placement selects which opposite corner pair gets the horizontal edge segments.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7c66cb00\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nDock two-tone sprites into color-matched horizontal bars with a color swap.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Top area: small two-tone sprites (outer color + inner color).\n- Below: horizontal bars spanning most of the row, each with a fill color and border color on edges.\n\nRule:\n- Match each sprite to the bar whose fill color equals the sprite's outer color.\n- Place the sprite at its original x-position, aligned to the bar's bottom.\n- Recolor: sprite outer → bar border, sprite inner → bar fill.\n- Erase original sprites from the top area.\n\nKey insight: Color matching is inverted — the sprite's dominant color identifies its target bar, then gets swapped to the bar's accent color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f8de559\" lines=\"19-37\">\n<description>\nErase the first border pixel hit by extending the red marker away from its pink trail.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant background surrounds a unique red marker and a four-pixel\n  pink trail.\n- The pink pixels form one straight 8-connected ray starting next to the\n  red marker.\n- In the opposite direction from that trail, the red marker faces a\n  non-background border or obstacle pixel beyond background cells.\n\nRule\n- Keep the whole grid fixed except for the first non-background pixel on\n  the ray from the red marker away from the pink trail.\n- Recolor that one hit pixel to the background color.\n\nKey insight\n- The pink trail marks where the red marker came from, so the edit is found\n  by ray-casting from the red marker in the opposite direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a416fc5b\" lines=\"95-106\">\n<description>\nAdd the two missing colored arms of a centered diamond-sprite motif, or clear a completed motif to a blank square.\n\nInput structure\nAn 11x11 board has orange background and pink separator bars forming nine 3x3 cells. Occupied cells contain a four-pixel diamond sprite; the center cell and one neighboring cell are red, and a completed board may also contain one grey and one teal sprite.\n\nRule\nWith only the two red sprites present, preserve the board and add grey and teal diamond sprites 135 degrees clockwise and counterclockwise from the non-center red arm. If those expected grey and teal sprites are already present, output an all-background square whose side length is the total number of sprite pixels.\n\nKey insight\nThe non-center red sprite is an eight-neighbor direction from the center; the other two colored arms are fixed rotations of that direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"973e499e\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nExpand each input pixel into a miniature same-color mask of the whole grid.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a non-empty two-dimensional grid of foreground colors.\n- Black is reserved for the empty/background pixels introduced in the expanded output.\n- The output has one input-shaped block for each input pixel.\n\nRule\n- For every input pixel with color c, replace that pixel by a block the same shape as the input.\n- Inside the block, write c exactly where the original input also has color c.\n- Write black everywhere else in the block.\n\nKey insight\nEach pixel selects the global mask of its own color; the result is the blockwise self-mask of the grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"902510d5\" lines=\"58-80\">\n<description>\nKeep the largest 8-connected motif and replace the corner/control pixels with a solid corner triangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Exactly one grid corner contains a foreground control pixel; it gives the\n  corner from which the triangle is painted.\n- There is one unique largest 8-connected monochrome foreground object, the\n  motif that remains in the output; all other foreground pixels are controls.\n- Every foreground component outside that motif is a singleton control pixel.\n- The fill color is the unique control color that appears in at least two\n  non-corner control pixels.\n\nRule\n- Start from a blank background canvas and redraw only the largest motif.\n- Count the non-corner control pixels; that count is the triangle side.\n- Paint the fill color on the right-triangle region where those two distances\n  from the marked corner's edges sum to less than the side length.\n\nKey insight\n- The scattered singleton controls are counted into a compact triangular\n  stack at the marked corner; only the largest motif is not part of the count.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ad3b40cf\" lines=\"72-87\">\n<description>\nMirror the smallest non-axis foreground color across the full straight axis.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One foreground color forms exactly one full row, column, main diagonal, or anti-diagonal reflection axis.\n- The remaining foreground colors include a unique least-populated color to copy.\n\nRule\n- Preserve the input grid.\n- Select every pixel of the unique smallest non-axis foreground color.\n- Reflect those pixels across the full straight axis and paint the reflected positions with the same color.\n\nKey insight\n- The axis color marks the mirror line, while the color population chooses which set of objects is duplicated.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"80a900e0\" lines=\"69-82\">\n<description>\nExtend diagonal rays outward from outer-border segments adjacent to diamond shapes on a checkerboard.\n\nInput structure:\n- Checkerboard bg (two most common colors).\n- Hollow diamond outlines (8-pixel) in a non-bg color (inner border).\n- Short diagonal line segments in another non-bg color adjacent to each diamond edge (outer border).\n\nRule:\n- For each outer-border segment, determine the outward direction (perpendicular to the segment, away from the diamond).\n- From each endpoint of the segment, cast a diagonal ray outward, painting over bg pixels with the segment's color until hitting a non-bg pixel or the grid edge.\n\nKey insight: Rays emit only from the two endpoints of each outer-border segment, not every pixel, and travel diagonally away from the diamond.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1190bc91\" lines=\"36-49\">\n<description>\nComplete a sparse grid by casting occluding diagonal rays from the longest run and filling the remaining diagonal-connected regions from the short bars.\n\nInput structure\n- Black-background grid with one unique longest 4-connected foreground object.\n- The longest object is a horizontal or vertical multicolor run; all other foreground objects are shorter monochrome bars.\n\nRule\n- On a blank canvas, process the longest run from left to right or top to bottom; each pixel emits four 45-degree rays that stop at the first already-painted pixel.\n- Each remaining monochrome bar fills every still-blank pixel reachable from it by diagonal steps.\n\nKey insight\n- Earlier pixels in the long run occlude later diagonal rays, and those ray barriers partition the background into diagonal-connected regions for the short bars.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"13f06aa5\" lines=\"36-51\">\n<description>\nExtend each colored marker from its directional sprite to a grid edge, painting a dotted ray and the whole edge side.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One repeated body color forms small 8-connected directional sprites.\n- Each marker is a singleton non-background, non-body color embedded in one sprite.\n\nRule:\n- The body pixels around a marker lie mostly behind it; the opposite direction is the marker's ray direction.\n- Paint every second pixel from the marker along that ray, then paint the entire boundary side reached by the ray.\n- If two painted boundary sides meet at a corner, make that shared corner black.\n\nKey insight:\n- The same seven-pixel body sprite rotates to encode the direction; its local center-of-mass bias points away from the ray.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"88bcf3b4\" lines=\"229-241\">\n<description>\nSwing an irregular line segment around a nearby obstacle sprite, preserving its pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, bar color (straight lines perpendicular to the grid edge, one end on the border), segment color (irregular stroke attached to bar's inner endpoint), obstacle color (sprite near the bar terminal).\n\nRule:\n- Each bar is perpendicular to the edge it touches; its inner (terminal) endpoint has an attached irregular segment.\n- The segment detaches and re-routes: it travels from the terminal toward the nearest obstacle, hugs the obstacle's boundary, then exits at a 45° diagonal.\n- Total painted pixels equal the original segment length.\n\nKey insight: The segment behaves like a rope swung around the obstacle — attach at the nearest point, trace the skin, exit diagonally, conserving length.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"58c02a16\" lines=\"10-26\">\n<description>\nExpand the top-left seed mask into a recursive colored pattern and tile it across the full grid.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- All foreground lies in a top-left rectangle whose last row and last column are a solid secondary color.\n- Removing that secondary-color row and column leaves a seed region containing background plus one primary color.\n\nRule\n- Use the primary-color pixels in the seed as a binary mask.\n- Replace each primary seed cell with a copy of the seed mask in the primary color, and each background seed cell\n  with a copy of the seed mask in the secondary color.\n- Repeat and crop that expanded tile to the original grid shape.\n\nKey insight\nThe solid L-shaped secondary-color border is a legend, not output content; it supplies the color used when the\nseed's background cells are expanded.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9caf5b84\" lines=\"28-45\">\n<description>\nRecolor every pixel whose color is not one of the two most frequent input colors to orange.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
